# General Engineering Standards

Applies to every language in this repository. Lane detail: @lanes/general/CONVENTIONS.md

## Change safety

- **Touch only what the task requires.** A drive-by fix in an unrelated file is scope creep, even
  when it is correct. Note it, finish the task, raise it separately.
- **Read the call path before changing a link in it.** If a signature changes, find every call
  site. Not most of them.
- **Prefer additive over destructive.** A new function beside the old one, then a switchover, beats
  editing a function twelve things depend on.
- **Leave the tree buildable.** Every commit compiles and every test runs.

## Errors

- Never swallow an error silently. An empty catch — `catch {}`, `catch (_: Exception) {}`,
  `except: pass` — is a red flag in every language and a finding at audit.
- Handle errors at the boundary that can actually do something about them.
- Error messages say what failed, what was expected, and what to try. No bare "Error".
- Fail loudly in development, degrade gracefully in production.

## Naming

- Names say what a thing **is** or **does**, not how it was implemented.
- No abbreviations except genuinely universal ones (`id`, `url`, `http`).
- Booleans read as assertions: `isLoading`, `hasAccess`, `canRetry`.
- Consistency inside a codebase beats correctness in the abstract — match what is already there.

## Boundaries

- Validate at the edge — user input, network responses, file contents, environment variables.
- Once inside a boundary, trust the type. Do not re-validate the same thing at five layers.
- No secrets in source, ever. Read from environment; commit an `.example` file, never the real one.
- Log what happened. Never log a token, key, password, or full request body containing either.

## Dependencies

- Prefer the standard library. A dependency is a permanent maintenance obligation.
- Before adding one: is it maintained, what does it pull in, what happens when it is abandoned?
- Adding a dependency is a **G2 design decision**, not a build detail. It gets named and approved.

## Comments

- Comment **why**, never what. The code says what.
- A comment explaining a workaround must name what it works around.
- Delete commented-out code. Git remembers it; the file should not.
